# ---- Atmosphere backend configuration ----
# Copy to .env and adjust. The server also has safe defaults baked in for the
# public Supabase values, so it runs without a .env in development.

# Server
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=4000

# Comma-separated list of allowed browser origins (the frontend dev server).
FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# ---- Supabase ----
# Project URL and PUBLISHABLE (anon) key are safe to expose to browsers.
SUPABASE_URL=https://ccxatzfsvzetciiwsjlj.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=sb_publishable_4ppzqtXQPeVPuzP8Ant-pQ_MZIPMcGn

# SERVICE ROLE key — SERVER-ONLY SECRET. Email/password login does not need it,
# but PIN sign-in DOES: unlocking mints a fresh session for an already-enrolled
# device, which requires admin privileges. Leave it unset and the PIN feature
# simply stays hidden — the app still works, users just always type a password.
# NEVER expose this to the browser and NEVER commit a real value.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ---- Device PIN ----
# Server-only secret mixed into every PIN hash. It must NOT live in the database
# — that separation is what stops a 4-digit PIN from being swept offline if the
# database is ever leaked. Generate with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# Required in production; development falls back to a well-known placeholder.
# Changing it invalidates every enrolled device, which is what you want if it
# is ever exposed.
# DEVICE_PEPPER=

# ---- Password reset ----
# Where the recovery email sends the user back to. Defaults to
# <FRONTEND_ORIGIN>/reset-password. This URL must ALSO be added to the Supabase
# dashboard under Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs, or the
# link in the email will be rejected.
# PASSWORD_RESET_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:5173/reset-password

# ---- Mitigation Estimator / Xactimate ----
# Which driver reaches Xactimate:
#   mock  (default) — a local stand-in. No network. The whole estimator, the
#                     consent flow and price-list reconciliation all work, so the
#                     feature is fully exercisable without anyone typing a real
#                     Xactimate password into a dev machine.
#   api             — Verisk's partner API. The best option if you have an
#                     integration agreement: supported, stable, and it never
#                     replays a password.
#   web             — browser automation of Xactimate Online. For orgs without
#                     API access. Read the notes on XACTIMATE_WEB_AUTOMATION.
# XACTIMATE_DRIVER=mock

# Partner API credentials. Required when XACTIMATE_DRIVER=api; the driver
# refuses to start without them rather than guessing at endpoints.
# XACTIMATE_API_BASE_URL=
# XACTIMATE_API_KEY=

# Browser automation is OFF unless you turn it on here. Whether automating a
# given Xactimate account is permitted depends on that account's terms with
# Verisk — that is the account holder's call, so it takes a deliberate act
# rather than a default. It also replays a password, which the API driver
# never does.
# XACTIMATE_WEB_AUTOMATION=false
# XACTIMATE_HEADLESS=true
# Xactimate Online's DOM is not a public interface. Override selectors here when
# the UI moves, so a break is a config edit and not a redeploy. JSON object,
# keys as in DEFAULT_WEB_SELECTORS (src/estimator/xactimate/webDriver.ts).
# XACTIMATE_WEB_SELECTORS={"usernameInput":"#user"}

# Server-only key for the Xactimate credential vault. Like DEVICE_PEPPER it must
# NEVER reach the database — a password that has to be replayed into a login form
# cannot be hashed, so key custody is the entire protection. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
# Leave it UNSET and at-rest storage is simply unavailable: users connect in
# session-only mode, their password is used once and never written down, and a
# database leak yields nothing because there is nothing there. That is the safer
# configuration — only set this if you need unattended runs (a nightly price
# sync, a scheduled submission) that cannot prompt for a password.
# XACTIMATE_ENC_KEY=

# ---- Carrier program agreements (SLA) ----
# Where the terms a franchise must estimate to come from:
#   manual (default) — someone reads the agreement and enters its terms through
#                      the app. Needs no credentials and is the ONLY source
#                      guaranteed to match what your franchise actually signed.
#   portal           — pulled from a franchisor contractor-portal endpoint that
#                      speaks the documented JSON contract (see
#                      src/estimator/carrier/source.ts). Review what comes back
#                      before saving it — a portal response that silently became
#                      your binding terms is a bad way to find out an endpoint
#                      changed shape.
#   mock             — representative demo terms, for development.
# There is deliberately NO browser scraper for a franchisor portal: a scraper
# written against markup nobody here has seen would not fail loudly when the page
# changed, it would return plausible-but-wrong contract terms, and an estimate
# built on a wrong equipment cap is worse than one built on no cap at all.
# SLA_SOURCE=manual
# SLA_PORTAL_BASE_URL=
# SLA_PORTAL_API_KEY=

# ---- Cookies ----
# In production behind HTTPS, cookies are automatically marked Secure.
# If the frontend and backend are on different sites, set:
#   COOKIE_SAMESITE=none   (and you MUST serve both over HTTPS)
# COOKIE_SAMESITE=lax
# COOKIE_DOMAIN=
